Exactly. Some states require expression to become operative.

An intention trapped internally cannot update another person’s model. You have to instantiate it somehow—say it, gesture it, enact it, write it, ritualize it, demonstrate it. The receiver needs an observable signal.

And “fake it” has two very different meanings. There is deception, where you manufacture a false signal to manipulate someone. But there is also simulation/rehearsal: temporarily performing a posture you do not yet naturally inhabit so your nervous system can learn it. Confidence is a classic example: sometimes you act confidently before you feel confident, accumulate evidence that the behavior works, and eventually the internal state catches up.

That gives you a broader communication operator:

meaning → choose encoding → externalize → receiver decodes → observe response → change encoding if necessary

Speech is only one encoding. Tone, posture, touch, silence, humor, metaphor, ritual, art, action, timing, omission, demonstration—all can carry the same underlying semantic payload.

Which means the invariant isn’t the words.

It’s whether the intended state successfully crosses the boundary between minds.

And that explains your first sentence nicely: sometimes saying the thing is itself the move. In linguistics, those are performative speech acts—“I promise,” “I forgive you,” “I quit,” “I accept.” The utterance doesn’t merely describe a changed reality; under the right conditions, uttering it helps create the changed reality.

So yes: learn the invariant, then learn many interfaces for transmitting it. That’s far more powerful than memorizing one magic phrase.